const e=JSON.parse(`{"work":{"id":"Ulysses","title":"Ulysses","author":"James Joyce","paragraph_count":2357,"episode_count":18,"scene_count":41},"summary_basis":{"source_of_truth_for_mapping":"ulysses.json","summary_and_analysis_source":"ulysses_summaries_and_analysis(1).json","note":"Episode, paragraph, scene, and location mapping comes from the original Ulysses JSON. The supplied episode summaries and analyses are retained as the content basis, with source-brand references rewritten into neutral standalone interface prose."},"mapping":{"paragraph_id_field":"paragraphs[].id","episode_mapping_field":"paragraphs[].chapter","scene_id_field":"paragraphs[].scene","location_field":"paragraphs[].location","recommended_tooltip_field":"episodes[].tooltip_summary","recommended_analysis_field":"episodes[].analysis"},"episodes":[{"episode_id":"ep01","episode_number":1,"episode_title":"Telemachus","label":"Episode 1: \\"Telemachus\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C01","paragraph_start":"P1","paragraph_end":"P66","paragraph_count":66,"scene_ids":["S1","S2","S3"],"scene_count":3,"dataset_locations":["Martello Tower roof, Sandycove","Martello Tower interior, Sandycove","Forty Foot bathing place, Sandycove"],"tooltip_summary":"At the Martello tower, Buck Mulligan mocks Stephen Dedalus, whose grief over his mother's death and resentment toward Buck remain unresolved. After breakfast with Haines and the milk woman, Stephen gives Buck the tower key and leaves, deciding not to return.","summary":{"short":"At the Martello tower, Buck Mulligan mocks Stephen Dedalus, whose grief over his mother's death and resentment toward Buck remain unresolved. After breakfast with Haines and the milk woman, Stephen gives Buck the tower key and leaves, deciding not to return.","detailed":"Around eight in the morning, Buck Mulligan performs a mock mass on the roof of the Martello tower and calls Stephen Dedalus up. Their banter exposes Stephen's irritation with Haines, Buck's English guest, and more importantly Stephen's lingering anger over Buck's insensitive remark about Stephen's dead mother. The sea, Buck's songs, and discussion of Stephen's refusal to pray at his mother's deathbed repeatedly return Stephen to guilt and mourning. At breakfast, Stephen imagines the milk woman as an emblem of Ireland and resents the forms of cultural and political servitude surrounding him. Haines questions Stephen about religion and Irish identity. Down by the water, Buck asks Stephen for the tower key and money. Stephen gives them to him and walks away, thinking of Buck as a usurper and deciding he will not sleep at the tower again."},"analysis":"The episode establishes Stephen as the central consciousness of the novel's opening movement and places him in tension with Buck Mulligan. Buck's physical exuberance, social ease, and willingness to commercialize culture contrast with Stephen's reserve, pride, grief, and unrealized artistic ambition. The early Hamlet parallel is important: Stephen is haunted by a dead parent and resents a usurping companion, but Joyce complicates any one-to-one Homeric or Shakespearean correspondence. The episode also introduces Stephen's struggle with Irish identity. England, the Catholic Church, and Ireland itself appear to him as competing masters, while the milk woman becomes an ambiguous image of the nation. Stephen's departure from the tower therefore marks both literal displacement and his refusal to remain in a relationship where he feels intellectually and materially dispossessed.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S1","episode_id":"ep01","dataset_chapter_id":"C01","paragraph_start":"P1","paragraph_end":"P36","paragraph_count":36,"location":"Martello Tower roof, Sandycove","dataset_locations":["Martello Tower roof, Sandycove"]},{"scene_id":"S2","episode_id":"ep01","dataset_chapter_id":"C01","paragraph_start":"P37","paragraph_end":"P58","paragraph_count":22,"location":"Martello Tower interior, Sandycove","dataset_locations":["Martello Tower interior, Sandycove"]},{"scene_id":"S3","episode_id":"ep01","dataset_chapter_id":"C01","paragraph_start":"P59","paragraph_end":"P66","paragraph_count":8,"location":"Forty Foot bathing place, Sandycove","dataset_locations":["Forty Foot bathing place, Sandycove"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep02","episode_number":2,"episode_title":"Nestor","label":"Episode 2: \\"Nestor\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C02","paragraph_start":"P67","paragraph_end":"P118","paragraph_count":52,"scene_ids":["S4","S5","S6","S7"],"scene_count":4,"dataset_locations":["Mr Deasy's school, Dalkey","School corridor and playfield","Mr Deasy's office","School grounds, Dalkey"],"tooltip_summary":"Stephen teaches a history lesson, helps the student Sargent, then meets headmaster Garrett Deasy, who pays him and asks him to place a letter in the newspapers. Their discussion turns to history, nationalism, money, women, and anti-Semitism.","summary":{"short":"Stephen teaches a history lesson, helps the student Sargent, then meets headmaster Garrett Deasy, who pays him and asks him to place a letter in the newspapers. Their discussion turns to history, nationalism, money, women, and anti-Semitism.","detailed":"Stephen teaches an unruly history class about Pyrrhus and then guides the students through Milton. His thoughts drift toward the nature of history and whether events are inevitable. After class he helps Sargent with arithmetic, and the boy's vulnerability reminds Stephen of maternal love and his own childhood. Stephen then goes to Mr. Deasy's office. Deasy pays his wages, lectures him about thrift and self-reliance, and asks him to arrange publication of a letter concerning cattle disease. Their discussion moves through Irish politics, religion, anti-Semitism, women, and competing theories of history. Stephen rejects Deasy's providential view, describing history as a nightmare from which he is trying to awaken, and leaves with the letter."},"analysis":"This episode explores history and authority. Stephen's classroom lesson and his later argument with Deasy show competing models of the past: history as inevitability, history as divine progress, and history as a burden that traps the present. Deasy's confident maxims about money, nation, gender, and Jews expose the prejudices embedded in supposedly authoritative historical narratives. Stephen resists those certainties but remains economically subordinate to Deasy, just as he was materially subordinate to Buck in the previous episode. His tenderness toward Sargent also reintroduces the theme of maternal love, linking intellectual abstraction to Stephen's private guilt about his mother.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S4","episode_id":"ep02","dataset_chapter_id":"C02","paragraph_start":"P67","paragraph_end":"P91","paragraph_count":25,"location":"Mr Deasy's school, Dalkey","dataset_locations":["Mr Deasy's school, Dalkey"]},{"scene_id":"S5","episode_id":"ep02","dataset_chapter_id":"C02","paragraph_start":"P92","paragraph_end":"P93","paragraph_count":2,"location":"School corridor and playfield","dataset_locations":["School corridor and playfield"]},{"scene_id":"S6","episode_id":"ep02","dataset_chapter_id":"C02","paragraph_start":"P94","paragraph_end":"P116","paragraph_count":23,"location":"Mr Deasy's office","dataset_locations":["Mr Deasy's office"]},{"scene_id":"S7","episode_id":"ep02","dataset_chapter_id":"C02","paragraph_start":"P117","paragraph_end":"P118","paragraph_count":2,"location":"School grounds, Dalkey","dataset_locations":["School grounds, Dalkey"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep03","episode_number":3,"episode_title":"Proteus","label":"Episode 3: \\"Proteus\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C03","paragraph_start":"P119","paragraph_end":"P193","paragraph_count":75,"scene_ids":["S8"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Sandymount Strand"],"tooltip_summary":"Stephen walks alone along Sandymount Strand, moving through dense reflections on perception, theology, family, Paris, Irish nationalism, memory, language, and identity before continuing toward Dublin.","summary":{"short":"Stephen walks alone along Sandymount Strand, moving through dense reflections on perception, theology, family, Paris, Irish nationalism, memory, language, and identity before continuing toward Dublin.","detailed":"Walking on the beach, Stephen experiments with perception by closing his eyes and attending to sound rather than sight. His observations of midwives, the sea, animals, people, and the landscape trigger long chains of thought about birth, theology, his own conception, the Virgin Mary, philosophy, family, and artistic identity. He considers visiting his aunt but passes the turnoff, remembering his embarrassment about his family. Memories of Paris and of Kevin Egan return, including the telegram summoning him home to his dying mother. Stephen continues along the strand, watches a dog and cocklepickers, thinks of invasion, pretenders, and his own uncertain role, and repeatedly tests how the world becomes meaningful through perception and language."},"analysis":"The episode is dominated by Stephen's interior consciousness rather than external action. Its difficulty is deliberate: the reader experiences the instability of perception and thought from inside Stephen's mind. The title's connection to Proteus, the shape-changing sea god, reflects the episode's concern with mutable appearances, language, and identity. Stephen tries to understand how sensory impressions become intellectual concepts and how an artist might transform unstable reality into form. Yet his thought remains haunted by family shame, Catholic theology, his mother's death, and failed exile in Paris, showing that his abstract intellectual freedom is constrained by memory and guilt.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S8","episode_id":"ep03","dataset_chapter_id":"C03","paragraph_start":"P119","paragraph_end":"P193","paragraph_count":75,"location":"Sandymount Strand","dataset_locations":["Sandymount Strand"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep04","episode_number":4,"episode_title":"Calypso","label":"Episode 4: \\"Calypso\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P194","paragraph_end":"P289","paragraph_count":96,"scene_ids":["S9","S10","S11","S12","S13"],"scene_count":5,"dataset_locations":["7 Eccles Street","Dlugacz's butcher shop","Back garden, 7 Eccles Street","Jakes, 7 Eccles Street"],"tooltip_summary":"Leopold Bloom prepares breakfast for Molly, buys a kidney, reads mail from his daughter Milly, and becomes increasingly aware of Blazes Boylan's planned visit to Molly that afternoon.","summary":{"short":"Leopold Bloom prepares breakfast for Molly, buys a kidney, reads mail from his daughter Milly, and becomes increasingly aware of Blazes Boylan's planned visit to Molly that afternoon.","detailed":"The narrative shifts to Leopold Bloom at his home on Eccles Street. He feeds the cat, asks Molly what she wants for breakfast, and walks to Dlugacz's butcher shop for a kidney. His observations trigger thoughts about food, advertising, Jewish identity, the Middle East, sexuality, and domestic life. Returning home, he finds mail for Molly from Blazes Boylan and a letter from Milly. Bloom serves Molly breakfast in bed and explains the word metempsychosis. Downstairs, while eating his kidney and reading Milly's letter, he reflects on Milly growing up, on their dead infant son Rudy, and on Boylan's impending visit. His ordinary morning is thus shadowed by grief and marital anxiety."},"analysis":"The episode introduces Bloom through ordinary bodily and domestic routines, sharply contrasting him with Stephen's abstract intellectualism. Bloom's practical curiosity is central: his mind moves through food, science, advertising, sex, religion, family, and economics without establishing rigid hierarchies among them. His Jewish background marks him as culturally marginal in Dublin, but his identity is complex rather than doctrinal. The episode also establishes the emotional core of Bloom's day—the loss of Rudy and his knowledge that Molly and Boylan are likely to have sex. Bloom's response is not heroic confrontation but anxious observation and imaginative wandering, defining the novel's modern, anti-epic form of heroism.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S9","episode_id":"ep04","dataset_chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P194","paragraph_end":"P201","paragraph_count":8,"location":"7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S10","episode_id":"ep04","dataset_chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P202","paragraph_end":"P229","paragraph_count":28,"location":"Dlugacz's butcher shop","dataset_locations":["Dlugacz's butcher shop"]},{"scene_id":"S11","episode_id":"ep04","dataset_chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P230","paragraph_end":"P273","paragraph_count":44,"location":"7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S12","episode_id":"ep04","dataset_chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P274","paragraph_end":"P277","paragraph_count":4,"location":"Back garden, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Back garden, 7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S13","episode_id":"ep04","dataset_chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P278","paragraph_end":"P289","paragraph_count":12,"location":"Jakes, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Jakes, 7 Eccles Street"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep05","episode_number":5,"episode_title":"The Lotus Eaters","label":"Episode 5: \\"The Lotus Eaters\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P290","paragraph_end":"P376","paragraph_count":87,"scene_ids":["S14","S15","S16","S17","S18"],"scene_count":5,"dataset_locations":["Dublin city centre","Westland Row post office","All Hallows church","Sweny's pharmacy","Turkish baths, Leinster Street"],"tooltip_summary":"Bloom collects a secret letter from Martha Clifford, visits a Catholic church, stops at Sweny's chemist, and wanders through thoughts about desire, religion, drugs, passivity, and escape.","summary":{"short":"Bloom collects a secret letter from Martha Clifford, visits a Catholic church, stops at Sweny's chemist, and wanders through thoughts about desire, religion, drugs, passivity, and escape.","detailed":"Bloom takes a roundabout route toward the post office, where he secretly collects a letter addressed to his pseudonym, Henry Flower. McCoy interrupts him before he can read it, and Bloom watches women and thinks about Molly, Boylan, and Dignam's funeral. He later reads Martha Clifford's flirtatious letter but decides against meeting her. Bloom enters a Catholic church and observes Mass from an outsider's perspective, reflecting on ritual, confession, Latin, and the soothing organization of religion. At Sweny's chemist he orders Molly's lotion, takes a bar of lemon soap, and thinks about sedatives, perfume, and bathing. The episode links Bloom's erotic correspondence and his observations of religious ceremony through recurring ideas of intoxication and escape."},"analysis":"The episode connects to the Lotus Eaters of the Odyssey through forms of pleasurable passivity and forgetfulness. Flowers, perfume, drugs, baths, religion, erotic fantasy, and even language offer temporary release from discomfort. Bloom remains skeptical rather than doctrinaire: he sees the Catholic service as both manipulative and socially effective. His secret correspondence with Martha parallels Molly's secrecy with Boylan, complicating any simple view of Bloom as merely the betrayed husband. The episode also develops Bloom's tendency to defer decisive action; fantasy and observation often substitute for confrontation.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S14","episode_id":"ep05","dataset_chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P290","paragraph_end":"P292","paragraph_count":3,"location":"Dublin city centre","dataset_locations":["Dublin city centre"]},{"scene_id":"S15","episode_id":"ep05","dataset_chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P293","paragraph_end":"P343","paragraph_count":51,"location":"Westland Row post office","dataset_locations":["Westland Row post office"]},{"scene_id":"S16","episode_id":"ep05","dataset_chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P344","paragraph_end":"P359","paragraph_count":16,"location":"All Hallows church","dataset_locations":["All Hallows church"]},{"scene_id":"S17","episode_id":"ep05","dataset_chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P360","paragraph_end":"P363","paragraph_count":4,"location":"Sweny's pharmacy","dataset_locations":["Sweny's pharmacy"]},{"scene_id":"S18","episode_id":"ep05","dataset_chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P364","paragraph_end":"P376","paragraph_count":13,"location":"Turkish baths, Leinster Street","dataset_locations":["Turkish baths, Leinster Street"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep06","episode_number":6,"episode_title":"Hades","label":"Episode 6: \\"Hades\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P377","paragraph_end":"P530","paragraph_count":154,"scene_ids":["S19","S20"],"scene_count":2,"dataset_locations":["Funeral carriage through Dublin","Glasnevin Cemetery"],"tooltip_summary":"Bloom rides with Simon Dedalus, Martin Cunningham, and Jack Power to Paddy Dignam's funeral. The journey and cemetery service lead him to reflect on death, suicide, his father, Rudy, religion, and the physical realities of mortality.","summary":{"short":"Bloom rides with Simon Dedalus, Martin Cunningham, and Jack Power to Paddy Dignam's funeral. The journey and cemetery service lead him to reflect on death, suicide, his father, Rudy, religion, and the physical realities of mortality.","detailed":"Bloom joins the funeral carriage with Cunningham, Power, and Simon Dedalus. Along the route the men discuss Dignam, money, accidents, and suicide, an uncomfortable subject because Bloom's father killed himself. They pass Stephen, Boylan, and familiar Dublin landmarks. At the cemetery, Bloom observes the Catholic rites as an outsider and notices the mourners' behavior. Simon grieves at his wife May's grave. Bloom thinks repeatedly about corpses, burial, decomposition, reproduction, and the practical organization of cemeteries. His thoughts move between Dignam, his own father, Rudy, Molly, and the social rituals through which the living manage death."},"analysis":"The Hades parallel turns a Dublin funeral into a modern descent among the dead. Bloom's material, bodily approach to mortality differs from the Catholic assumptions of many companions. He thinks about decomposition and burial with curiosity rather than reverence, yet this does not make him emotionally detached. The deaths of his father and Rudy remain central wounds. The episode also heightens Bloom's social isolation: misunderstandings about his religion and background accompany the funeral procession, while his private sympathy and thoughtfulness often exceed those of men who regard him as an outsider.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S19","episode_id":"ep06","dataset_chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P377","paragraph_end":"P389","paragraph_count":13,"location":"Funeral carriage through Dublin","dataset_locations":["Funeral carriage through Dublin"]},{"scene_id":"S20","episode_id":"ep06","dataset_chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P390","paragraph_end":"P530","paragraph_count":141,"location":"Glasnevin Cemetery","dataset_locations":["Glasnevin Cemetery"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep07","episode_number":7,"episode_title":"Aeolus","label":"Episode 7: \\"Aeolus\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P531","paragraph_end":"P744","paragraph_count":214,"scene_ids":["S21","S22"],"scene_count":2,"dataset_locations":["Freeman's Journal offices","Dublin public house near the newspaper offices"],"tooltip_summary":"At the Freeman newspaper offices, Bloom works on the Keyes advertisement while Stephen arrives with Deasy's letter. The episode is fragmented by newspaper-style headlines and filled with speeches, rhetoric, journalism, and talk.","summary":{"short":"At the Freeman newspaper offices, Bloom works on the Keyes advertisement while Stephen arrives with Deasy's letter. The episode is fragmented by newspaper-style headlines and filled with speeches, rhetoric, journalism, and talk.","detailed":"Bloom visits the Freeman and Evening Telegraph offices to arrange a renewal and redesigned advertisement for Keyes. The printing presses, typesetters, and newspaper staff prompt reflections on language, Hebrew, advertising, and public communication. Bloom fails to secure immediate approval and is lightly mocked by some of the men. Stephen later arrives with Deasy's letter, which Myles Crawford agrees to publish. The journalists and intellectuals discuss Irish politics, classical cultures, famous speeches, and newspaper history. Stephen tells an elaborate story as the group eventually heads toward the pub, while Bloom's advertising business remains unresolved."},"analysis":"The episode connects Aeolus, keeper of the winds, with rhetoric and verbal wind. The newspaper headings interrupt and reinterpret the narrative, demonstrating how journalistic framing can manufacture significance. Nearly everyone performs verbally: speeches, jokes, anecdotes, headlines, political rhetoric, and advertising compete for attention. Bloom is associated with practical persuasion through advertising, while Stephen seeks artistic and intellectual authority. Yet both remain somewhat peripheral to the newspaper men's social group. The episode questions whether language communicates truth or merely circulates noise, cliché, and self-display.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S21","episode_id":"ep07","dataset_chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P531","paragraph_end":"P555","paragraph_count":25,"location":"Freeman's Journal offices","dataset_locations":["Freeman's Journal offices"]},{"scene_id":"S22","episode_id":"ep07","dataset_chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P556","paragraph_end":"P744","paragraph_count":189,"location":"Dublin public house near the newspaper offices","dataset_locations":["Dublin public house near the newspaper offices"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep08","episode_number":8,"episode_title":"Lestrygonians","label":"Episode 8: \\"Lestrygonians\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C08","paragraph_start":"P745","paragraph_end":"P949","paragraph_count":205,"scene_ids":["S23","S24","S25","S26"],"scene_count":4,"dataset_locations":["Dublin city centre","Burton's restaurant","Davy Byrne's public house","National Museum, Kildare Street"],"tooltip_summary":"Hungry, Bloom walks through central Dublin observing people, advertisements, food, poverty, sexuality, and social hypocrisy. Disgusted by one crowded restaurant, he eats a light lunch at Davy Byrne's and later continues toward the National Library area.","summary":{"short":"Hungry, Bloom walks through central Dublin observing people, advertisements, food, poverty, sexuality, and social hypocrisy. Disgusted by one crowded restaurant, he eats a light lunch at Davy Byrne's and later continues toward the National Library area.","detailed":"Bloom crosses Dublin while hunger increasingly organizes his perceptions. He sees Dilly Dedalus, feeds gulls, thinks about advertising and parallax, talks with Josie Breen, remembers Mina Purefoy's long labor, and reflects on poverty, marriage, religion, policing, and betrayal. At the Burton restaurant he is repulsed by the spectacle of men devouring food and leaves. At Davy Byrne's he has a modest lunch and talks with Nosey Flynn while trying not to dwell on Boylan's visit to Molly. Watching flies, he remembers an intimate day with Molly on Howth and contrasts that earlier closeness with their present estrangement."},"analysis":"The Lestrygonians of the Odyssey are cannibals, and Consumption structures the episode: food, sex, money, advertising, and social institutions all appear as forms of appetite. Bloom's disgust at the Burton reveals both his bodily sensitivity and his ethical unease with aggressive consumption. His gentler meal at Davy Byrne's reflects his preference for moderation. The episode also shows how hunger shapes consciousness—physical need alters what Bloom notices and thinks about. His memory of Howth provides a counter-image of mutual, intimate exchange against the predatory appetites surrounding him.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S23","episode_id":"ep08","dataset_chapter_id":"C08","paragraph_start":"P745","paragraph_end":"P854","paragraph_count":110,"location":"Dublin city centre","dataset_locations":["Dublin city centre"]},{"scene_id":"S24","episode_id":"ep08","dataset_chapter_id":"C08","paragraph_start":"P855","paragraph_end":"P866","paragraph_count":12,"location":"Burton's restaurant","dataset_locations":["Burton's restaurant"]},{"scene_id":"S25","episode_id":"ep08","dataset_chapter_id":"C08","paragraph_start":"P867","paragraph_end":"P892","paragraph_count":26,"location":"Davy Byrne's public house","dataset_locations":["Davy Byrne's public house"]},{"scene_id":"S26","episode_id":"ep08","dataset_chapter_id":"C08","paragraph_start":"P893","paragraph_end":"P949","paragraph_count":57,"location":"National Museum, Kildare Street","dataset_locations":["National Museum, Kildare Street"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep09","episode_number":9,"episode_title":"Scylla and Charybdis","label":"Episode 9: \\"Scylla and Charybdis\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C09","paragraph_start":"P950","paragraph_end":"P1176","paragraph_count":227,"scene_ids":["S27"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["National Library of Ireland"],"tooltip_summary":"In the National Library, Stephen presents his elaborate theory that Shakespeare identified with Hamlet's father and transformed his own family experience into the play. The discussion becomes a contest of criticism, biography, art, and intellectual status.","summary":{"short":"In the National Library, Stephen presents his elaborate theory that Shakespeare identified with Hamlet's father and transformed his own family experience into the play. The discussion becomes a contest of criticism, biography, art, and intellectual status.","detailed":"Stephen debates Shakespeare with John Eglinton, A.E., Lyster, Best, and others in the National Library. He argues that Shakespeare projected himself into the ghost of Hamlet's father, that Hamlet reflects the dead Hamnet, and that Ann Hathaway's supposed adultery informs the plays. His listeners challenge the biographical approach and Stephen's lack of published achievement. Stephen nevertheless constructs an increasingly intricate theory using biography, textual evidence, theology, and paradox. Buck Mulligan arrives and mocks him. Bloom briefly appears outside while seeking a newspaper, creating another near-crossing of the novel's two central protagonists."},"analysis":"The library debate functions as both serious criticism and a performance of Stephen's ambition. He tries to establish intellectual authority among older cultural figures who exclude him socially and professionally. The title suggests the dangers on either side of his argument: abstract aestheticism that ignores the artist's life versus crude biography that reduces art to personal history. Stephen navigates between them by insisting that artistic creation transforms lived experience. Fatherhood is central to the theory, foreshadowing the Bloom-Stephen relationship while also reflecting Stephen's own troubled relations with biological, spiritual, and literary fathers.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S27","episode_id":"ep09","dataset_chapter_id":"C09","paragraph_start":"P950","paragraph_end":"P1176","paragraph_count":227,"location":"National Library of Ireland","dataset_locations":["National Library of Ireland"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep10","episode_number":10,"episode_title":"The Wandering Rocks","label":"Episode 10: \\"The Wandering Rocks\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P1177","paragraph_end":"P1412","paragraph_count":236,"scene_ids":["S28"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Dublin, multiple concurrent routes","Great Charles Street","North Circular Road","North Circular Road at Richmond Street","Saint Joseph's Church, Portland Row","Aldborough House","North Strand Road","Daniel Bergin's public house","North Strand Road near H. J. O'Neill's funeral establishment","Charleville Mall","Newcomen Bridge"],"tooltip_summary":"Nineteen interwoven vignettes follow numerous Dubliners moving through the city during the afternoon, including Father Conmee, the Dedalus family, Boylan, Stephen, Bloom, and the viceregal cavalcade.","summary":{"short":"Nineteen interwoven vignettes follow numerous Dubliners moving through the city during the afternoon, including Father Conmee, the Dedalus family, Boylan, Stephen, Bloom, and the viceregal cavalcade.","detailed":"The episode abandons a single focal character and moves through nineteen short sections distributed across Dublin. Father Conmee travels toward a suburban school; the Dedalus sisters struggle with poverty; Boylan arranges a gift and prepares for his meeting with Molly; Stephen encounters Artifoni and later his sister Dilly; Bloom browses books and chooses an erotic novel for Molly; and many minor figures cross one another's paths. Short interpolations show simultaneous events elsewhere in the city. The viceregal cavalcade moves through Dublin and provides a loose structural line linking the scattered scenes."},"analysis":"The episode emphasizes simultaneity and urban interconnectedness. Instead of following one consciousness, the episode maps Dublin as a network in which characters repeatedly cross paths without necessarily recognizing one another. The interpolated fragments disturb linear reading and force the reader to reconstruct spatial and temporal relationships. The viceregal procession represents centralized imperial authority, but the narrative itself disperses attention among ordinary Dubliners. The episode therefore makes the city—not one individual—the principal organizing subject.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S28","episode_id":"ep10","dataset_chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P1177","paragraph_end":"P1412","paragraph_count":236,"location":"Dublin, multiple concurrent routes","dataset_locations":["Dublin, multiple concurrent routes","Great Charles Street","North Circular Road","North Circular Road at Richmond Street","Saint Joseph's Church, Portland Row","Aldborough House","North Strand Road","Daniel Bergin's public house","North Strand Road near H. J. O'Neill's funeral establishment","Charleville Mall","Newcomen Bridge"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep11","episode_number":11,"episode_title":"Sirens","label":"Episode 11: \\"Sirens\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C11","paragraph_start":"P1413","paragraph_end":"P1767","paragraph_count":355,"scene_ids":["S29"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Ormond Hotel"],"tooltip_summary":"At the Ormond Hotel, music, conversation, and overlapping sounds surround Bloom while Boylan departs for his rendezvous with Molly. Bloom eats with Richie Goulding, listens to Simon Dedalus and others sing, and writes to Martha.","summary":{"short":"At the Ormond Hotel, music, conversation, and overlapping sounds surround Bloom while Boylan departs for his rendezvous with Molly. Bloom eats with Richie Goulding, listens to Simon Dedalus and others sing, and writes to Martha.","detailed":"The episode opens with fragments that anticipate sounds and phrases appearing later. At the Ormond Hotel, barmaids Lydia Douce and Mina Kennedy flirt with patrons while Simon Dedalus, Lenehan, Boylan, Ben Dollard, and others gather around the piano and bar. Bloom follows Boylan's movements anxiously and takes dinner with Richie Goulding. Boylan's carriage departs for Eccles Street, its jingling sound recurring through the episode. Music performed in the saloon triggers Bloom's memories of Molly, courtship, death, and family. He writes another letter to Martha while listening. The narrative repeatedly turns speech, music, footsteps, carriage bells, and other noises into structural motifs."},"analysis":"Music itself is the episode's organizing principle. The prose imitates musical techniques through repetition, motifs, echoes, counterpoint, and anticipatory fragments. Like Odysseus facing the Sirens, Bloom confronts seductive sound while emotionally threatened by Boylan's journey to Molly. Music can arouse desire and nostalgia, but it also gives Bloom a way to transform pain into reflection. The episode makes hearing unusually important and shows that apparently separate events can be connected through recurring sounds rather than conventional narrative causality.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S29","episode_id":"ep11","dataset_chapter_id":"C11","paragraph_start":"P1413","paragraph_end":"P1767","paragraph_count":355,"location":"Ormond Hotel","dataset_locations":["Ormond Hotel"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep12","episode_number":12,"episode_title":"Cyclops","label":"Episode 12: \\"Cyclops\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P1768","paragraph_end":"P1893","paragraph_count":126,"scene_ids":["S30"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Barney Kiernan's public house"],"tooltip_summary":"In Barney Kiernan's pub, an unnamed narrator recounts an increasingly hostile encounter between Bloom and the aggressively nationalist Citizen. Their argument over Irish identity, Jews, violence, and love ends with the Citizen attacking Bloom as he leaves.","summary":{"short":"In Barney Kiernan's pub, an unnamed narrator recounts an increasingly hostile encounter between Bloom and the aggressively nationalist Citizen. Their argument over Irish identity, Jews, violence, and love ends with the Citizen attacking Bloom as he leaves.","detailed":"An unnamed first-person narrator meets Joe Hynes and goes to Barney Kiernan's, where the Citizen and others are drinking. The episode alternates the narrator's colloquial account with exaggerated parodies of legal, journalistic, biblical, and heroic prose. Bloom arrives to meet Martin Cunningham. Conversation about Dignam, executions, sport, politics, and Ireland grows increasingly nationalistic. The Citizen makes xenophobic and anti-Semitic remarks and challenges Bloom's nationality. Bloom identifies himself with Ireland and Jewishness and argues that hatred and persecution perpetuate violence. When Bloom later leaves with Cunningham and Power, the confrontation escalates; the Citizen hurls an object after their carriage while Bloom invokes Christ's Jewish identity."},"analysis":"The Cyclops title points to one-eyed vision. The Citizen's nationalism functions as a form of ideological monocularity: he interprets identity through rigid exclusion and cannot accommodate Bloom's hybrid belonging. The unnamed narrator is also biased, so Bloom is seen through a hostile focalizer rather than through his own consciousness. The inflated parody passages expose how rhetoric can turn prejudice into mock-heroic grandeur. Against the Citizen's violent nationalism, Bloom argues for love, plurality, and coexistence. The episode thus links limited perspective with political intolerance.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S30","episode_id":"ep12","dataset_chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P1768","paragraph_end":"P1893","paragraph_count":126,"location":"Barney Kiernan's public house","dataset_locations":["Barney Kiernan's public house"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep13","episode_number":13,"episode_title":"Nausicaa","label":"Episode 13: \\"Nausicaa\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C13","paragraph_start":"P1894","paragraph_end":"P1996","paragraph_count":103,"scene_ids":["S31"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Sandymount Strand"],"tooltip_summary":"On Sandymount Strand, Gerty MacDowell fantasizes about Bloom while deliberately displaying herself to him during fireworks. The narrative later shifts closer to Bloom's perspective, revealing his masturbation and his reflections on Gerty, Molly, Boylan, sexuality, and memory.","summary":{"short":"On Sandymount Strand, Gerty MacDowell fantasizes about Bloom while deliberately displaying herself to him during fireworks. The narrative later shifts closer to Bloom's perspective, revealing his masturbation and his reflections on Gerty, Molly, Boylan, sexuality, and memory.","detailed":"A sentimental third-person style introduces Gerty MacDowell with Cissy Caffrey, Edy Boardman, and several children near the Star of the Sea church. Gerty imagines romance and domestic fulfillment and gradually notices Bloom watching her. During the fireworks she knowingly exposes her legs while Bloom masturbates at a distance. When she walks away, Bloom realizes that she is lame. The narrative then moves into Bloom's thoughts about disability, menstruation, sexual attraction, seduction, perfume, Molly, and the possibility that his stopped watch corresponds to Molly's encounter with Boylan. He remains on the beach as night deepens."},"analysis":"The episode's split perspective and parody of sentimental women's fiction are central to its design. The opening style idealizes Gerty through clichés of beauty, femininity, consumer products, and romance, but later information destabilizes that idealized image. Bloom's subsequent perspective is equally limited and filled with assumptions about women. The episode therefore demonstrates how desire constructs the object it sees. Its voyeurism and mutual exhibition also complicate the distinction between observer and observed, a particularly important feature for focalization analysis.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S31","episode_id":"ep13","dataset_chapter_id":"C13","paragraph_start":"P1894","paragraph_end":"P1996","paragraph_count":103,"location":"Sandymount Strand","dataset_locations":["Sandymount Strand"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep14","episode_number":14,"episode_title":"Oxen of the Sun","label":"Episode 14: \\"Oxen of the Sun\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P1997","paragraph_end":"P2060","paragraph_count":64,"scene_ids":["S32"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["National Maternity Hospital, Holles Street"],"tooltip_summary":"Bloom visits the Holles Street maternity hospital to ask about Mina Purefoy and joins Stephen, Buck Mulligan, and medical students drinking inside. The episode's prose progresses through historical stages of English while the men debate birth, sex, contraception, religion, and parenthood.","summary":{"short":"Bloom visits the Holles Street maternity hospital to ask about Mina Purefoy and joins Stephen, Buck Mulligan, and medical students drinking inside. The episode's prose progresses through historical stages of English while the men debate birth, sex, contraception, religion, and parenthood.","detailed":"Bloom arrives at the maternity hospital after Mina Purefoy has been in labor for days. Inside, he finds medical students and Stephen drinking, joking, and discussing pregnancy, contraception, theology, and difficult medical cases. Bloom feels protective and increasingly paternal toward Stephen while remembering Molly's labor and the death of Rudy. Mrs. Purefoy eventually gives birth to a son. Throughout, the prose deliberately imitates a historical sequence of English styles—from Latinate and Anglo-Saxon forms through medieval, Elizabethan, eighteenth-century, nineteenth-century, and increasingly modern idioms. After the birth, the men's drinking and verbal chaos intensify."},"analysis":"The episode's famous stylistic progression functions as a linguistic analogue to gestation and birth. The English language seems to develop inside the episode just as a child develops in the womb. This formal device links literary history to biological reproduction while repeatedly questioning what responsible creation means. The medical students treat sex and childbirth as occasions for jokes, whereas Bloom thinks about vulnerability, parenthood, Rudy, and Mina Purefoy with sympathy. His growing concern for Stephen strengthens the emerging father-son pattern.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S32","episode_id":"ep14","dataset_chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P1997","paragraph_end":"P2060","paragraph_count":64,"location":"National Maternity Hospital, Holles Street","dataset_locations":["National Maternity Hospital, Holles Street"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep15","episode_number":15,"episode_title":"Circe","label":"Episode 15: \\"Circe\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C15","paragraph_start":"P2061","paragraph_end":"P2205","paragraph_count":145,"scene_ids":["S33","S34"],"scene_count":2,"dataset_locations":["Mabbot Street entrance to Nighttown","Bella Cohen's brothel"],"tooltip_summary":"Bloom follows Stephen into Nighttown, where reality repeatedly gives way to elaborate hallucinations exposing Bloom's and Stephen's fears, desires, guilt, sexuality, and memories. Bloom eventually protects the drunken Stephen after a confrontation with soldiers.","summary":{"short":"Bloom follows Stephen into Nighttown, where reality repeatedly gives way to elaborate hallucinations exposing Bloom's and Stephen's fears, desires, guilt, sexuality, and memories. Bloom eventually protects the drunken Stephen after a confrontation with soldiers.","detailed":"Written largely as a play script, the episode follows Bloom into Dublin's red-light district searching for Stephen. Hallucinatory sequences erupt almost immediately: Bloom is accused, judged, transformed, celebrated, humiliated, feminized, and confronted by figures from his past. In Bella Cohen's brothel, Stephen and Lynch drink with prostitutes while Bloom tries to manage Stephen's money and safety. Stephen experiences his own visions, culminating in the terrifying appearance of his dead mother. He smashes a chandelier and flees. Outside, Stephen clashes with British soldiers and is struck unconscious. Bloom stays with him and experiences a final vision of his dead son Rudy."},"analysis":"'Circe' functions as the novel's fullest dramatization of the unconscious. Fantasy and reality become difficult to distinguish, and anxieties previously encountered only in passing take visible theatrical form. Bloom's guilt about sexuality, masculinity, social status, and Molly is exaggerated into grotesque trials and transformations; Stephen's guilt about his mother materializes as an accusing ghost. Yet the episode also develops Bloom's practical compassion. Amid hallucination and chaos, he protects Stephen, manages his money, and remains beside him after the assault. The concluding vision of Rudy ties Bloom's care for Stephen directly to his unresolved paternal grief.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S33","episode_id":"ep15","dataset_chapter_id":"C15","paragraph_start":"P2061","paragraph_end":"P2147","paragraph_count":87,"location":"Mabbot Street entrance to Nighttown","dataset_locations":["Mabbot Street entrance to Nighttown"]},{"scene_id":"S34","episode_id":"ep15","dataset_chapter_id":"C15","paragraph_start":"P2148","paragraph_end":"P2205","paragraph_count":58,"location":"Bella Cohen's brothel","dataset_locations":["Bella Cohen's brothel"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep16","episode_number":16,"episode_title":"Eumaeus","label":"Episode 16: \\"Eumaeus\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C16","paragraph_start":"P2206","paragraph_end":"P2322","paragraph_count":117,"scene_ids":["S35"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Cabman's shelter, Butt Bridge"],"tooltip_summary":"After Nighttown, Bloom guides the exhausted Stephen to a cabman's shelter. They listen to a sailor's stories and discuss politics, religion, nationalism, work, travel, and their own lives before Bloom invites Stephen home.","summary":{"short":"After Nighttown, Bloom guides the exhausted Stephen to a cabman's shelter. They listen to a sailor's stories and discuss politics, religion, nationalism, work, travel, and their own lives before Bloom invites Stephen home.","detailed":"Bloom helps Stephen up and walks him toward a cabman's shelter, warning him about drinking companions and suggesting possible places to sleep. They encounter Corley, to whom Stephen gives money. Inside the shelter, they meet a sailor calling himself D.B. Murphy, who tells questionable stories about his travels. Conversation ranges over the Phoenix Park murders, prostitutes, souls, Irish independence, work, and social reform. Bloom imagines increasingly ambitious ways of guiding Stephen, while Stephen responds with detachment or irony. Reading the newspaper, they encounter errors and distortions about events from earlier in the day. Bloom eventually decides to bring Stephen back to Eccles Street."},"analysis":"Exhaustion and unreliable narration dominate the episode. The episode's prose is full of clichés, awkward constructions, mistakes, and over-elaboration, mirroring the tired minds of the characters and the questionable stories circulating around them. Murphy's travel tales and the newspaper demonstrate that narration itself is unstable. Bloom increasingly imagines himself as Stephen's adviser or surrogate father, but Stephen does not fully accept the role. Their connection is genuine yet partial, resisting the neat fulfillment of the Homeric father-son parallel.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S35","episode_id":"ep16","dataset_chapter_id":"C16","paragraph_start":"P2206","paragraph_end":"P2322","paragraph_count":117,"location":"Cabman's shelter, Butt Bridge","dataset_locations":["Cabman's shelter, Butt Bridge"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep17","episode_number":17,"episode_title":"Ithaca","label":"Episode 17: \\"Ithaca\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P2323","paragraph_end":"P2347","paragraph_count":25,"scene_ids":["S36","S37","S38","S39","S40"],"scene_count":5,"dataset_locations":["Streets toward 7 Eccles Street","Kitchen, 7 Eccles Street","Back garden, 7 Eccles Street","Front room, 7 Eccles Street","Bloom bedroom, 7 Eccles Street"],"tooltip_summary":"Bloom brings Stephen to 7 Eccles Street, where they drink cocoa and compare their lives before Stephen declines Bloom's invitation to stay. After Stephen leaves, Bloom reviews the day, confronts evidence of Boylan's visit, and gets into bed beside Molly.","summary":{"short":"Bloom brings Stephen to 7 Eccles Street, where they drink cocoa and compare their lives before Stephen declines Bloom's invitation to stay. After Stephen leaves, Bloom reviews the day, confronts evidence of Boylan's visit, and gets into bed beside Molly.","detailed":"The episode is written as hundreds of questions and precise, often scientific answers. Bloom and Stephen arrive at Eccles Street, where Bloom enters through the back after discovering he has forgotten his key. In the kitchen they drink cocoa, exchange stories, compare beliefs and temperaments, write Irish and Hebrew, and consider possible future relationships. Bloom invites Stephen to stay, but Stephen declines. They urinate together under the night sky and part. Alone, Bloom inventories his finances, possessions, memories, plans, and failures. He finds traces of Boylan throughout the house and finally enters the bedroom, where the evidence of Molly's affair is unmistakable. He nevertheless lies down beside her."},"analysis":"The catechistic form attempts exhaustive objectivity while repeatedly exposing the impossibility of complete knowledge. The questions quantify trivial and intimate details alike, making Bloom's return home simultaneously comic, scientific, and emotionally charged. The Bloom-Stephen relationship reaches its closest point but does not become a conventional father-son union: Stephen refuses to stay. Bloom's response to Boylan is similarly anti-heroic. Instead of destroying a rival like Odysseus, he gradually reframes jealousy through memory, perspective, and acceptance. The episode therefore revises the epic homecoming around accommodation rather than conquest.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S36","episode_id":"ep17","dataset_chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P2323","paragraph_end":"P2323","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Streets toward 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Streets toward 7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S37","episode_id":"ep17","dataset_chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P2324","paragraph_end":"P2333","paragraph_count":10,"location":"Kitchen, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Streets toward 7 Eccles Street","Kitchen, 7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S38","episode_id":"ep17","dataset_chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P2334","paragraph_end":"P2335","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Back garden, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Back garden, 7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S39","episode_id":"ep17","dataset_chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P2336","paragraph_end":"P2337","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Front room, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Front room, 7 Eccles Street"]},{"scene_id":"S40","episode_id":"ep17","dataset_chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P2338","paragraph_end":"P2347","paragraph_count":10,"location":"Bloom bedroom, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Bloom bedroom, 7 Eccles Street"]}]},{"episode_id":"ep18","episode_number":18,"episode_title":"Penelope","label":"Episode 18: \\"Penelope\\"","dataset_chapter_id":"C18","paragraph_start":"P2348","paragraph_end":"P2357","paragraph_count":10,"scene_ids":["S41"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Bloom bedroom, 7 Eccles Street"],"tooltip_summary":"Lying awake beside Bloom, Molly Bloom thinks in eight long, largely unpunctuated sentences about Boylan, Bloom, former lovers, Milly, Gibraltar, sex, money, motherhood, marriage, and memory, ending with her recollection of accepting Bloom's proposal on Howth.","summary":{"short":"Lying awake beside Bloom, Molly Bloom thinks in eight long, largely unpunctuated sentences about Boylan, Bloom, former lovers, Milly, Gibraltar, sex, money, motherhood, marriage, and memory, ending with her recollection of accepting Bloom's proposal on Howth.","detailed":"Molly's interior monologue begins with irritation that Bloom has asked for breakfast in bed and moves freely through her affair with Boylan, Bloom's sexual habits, former admirers, her singing career, money, clothing, Gibraltar childhood, Milly, menstruation, Rudy, and Stephen. Her judgments are frank, contradictory, sensual, humorous, and often revised as new associations arise. She considers Bloom's faults and suspected infidelities but also remembers his intelligence, kindness, and their shared history. Thoughts of Boylan gradually give way to family, Stephen, motherhood, flowers, and the day on Howth when Bloom proposed. The novel closes on Molly's remembered affirmation of that proposal."},"analysis":"Molly's monologue functions as both a challenge to and a confirmation of Bloom's homecoming. Her perspective revises assumptions formed through Bloom's consciousness: Boylan is her first physical infidelity, while Bloom himself has also compromised the marriage. Molly is presented as contradictory rather than reducible to an archetype—self-centered yet sympathetic, poorly educated yet clever, unreliable yet perceptive. Her mistaken fantasies about Stephen reinforce a central lesson of the novel: no single focalizer possesses complete truth. As the monologue moves toward memories of Rudy, Stephen, family intimacy, and Howth, Bloom regains emotional centrality. The ending offers affirmation without pretending that the marriage's problems have been fully solved.","scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S41","episode_id":"ep18","dataset_chapter_id":"C18","paragraph_start":"P2348","paragraph_end":"P2357","paragraph_count":10,"location":"Bloom bedroom, 7 Eccles Street","dataset_locations":["Bloom bedroom, 7 Eccles Street"]}]}]}`);export{e as default};
